    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Addis Ababa began in 1886 as a royal camp beside hot springs, laid out like an army on the march, and became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>'s empire in 1889.</li><li>Italian troops entered the city on 5 May 1936 and held it until 1941, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> returned on the fifth anniversary of his departure; later plans by Italian, British and French consultants shaped its growth.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> was deposed in 1974 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a> seized the city in 1991, and the capital became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> and its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The city was first laid out as a katama, a royal camp, with the Imperial Palace in the centre and the lands of the nobles placed on each side as an army is drawn up on the march.</p></aside>
    </section>

